('causal', 1, 'Module 1: The Counterfactual Problem',
  'What we mean when we say a programme caused an outcome, why the comparison we want can never be observed, and how the potential-outcomes model makes the problem precise.',
  $html$<h2>Module 1: The Counterfactual Problem</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Almost every claim in development practice is causal. A transfer cut stunting. A bridge raised attendance. A guarantee lifted rural wages. The difficulty is that the comparison which would settle such a claim cannot be run, and the comparison that <em>can</em> be run usually answers a different question. This module makes that problem precise, so that every later design can be read as one more attempt to solve it.</p></div>

<h3>The Comparison You Cannot Run</h3>
<p>Take one household that worked on an MGNREGA site this year. Call its consumption <em>Y</em>. To know whether the work <em>caused</em> that consumption, you would need to see the same household, in the same year, having <em>not</em> worked. That world does not exist. You observe one outcome, never both.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> The fundamental problem</div><p><strong>A causal effect is a comparison between two outcomes, only one of which you ever observe.</strong> This is not a small-sample problem that more data fixes; it is a missing-data problem built into the definition. Holland (1986) named it the <em>fundamental problem of causal inference</em>.</p></div>

<h3>The Potential-Outcomes Model</h3>
<p>Fix a unit <em>i</em> and a binary treatment <em>D<sub>i</sub></em> &isin; {0,1}. Define two <em>potential outcomes</em>: <span>\(Y_i(1)\)</span>, the outcome if <em>i</em> is treated, and <span>\(Y_i(0)\)</span>, the outcome if it is not. Each unit has both; reality reveals only one. This notation is due to Splawa-Neyman (1923) for experiments and Rubin (1974) for observational settings.</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ Y_i \;=\; D_i\,Y_i(1) \;+\; (1-D_i)\,Y_i(0) \]</div><div class="equation-caption">The observed outcome is a switch. For a treated unit the second term vanishes; for an untreated unit the first does. The other half is the counterfactual you must reconstruct.</div></div>
<p>The <em>individual treatment effect</em> is the difference between a unit's two potential outcomes,</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \tau_i \;=\; Y_i(1) - Y_i(0), \]</div><div class="equation-caption">which is never observed for anyone, because only one potential outcome is ever realised. Causal inference is therefore unavoidably about <em>averages</em>, not individuals.</div></div>

<h3>Three Estimands &mdash; Name Yours First</h3>
<p>Since individual effects are out of reach, we target averages. Three appear constantly, and they are <em>not</em> the same number:</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Estimands</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">ATE &mdash; average treatment effect</div><div class="definition-text">\( \text{ATE} = E[\,Y(1)-Y(0)\,] \), the effect averaged over <em>everyone</em>.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">ATT &mdash; effect on the treated</div><div class="definition-text">\( \text{ATT} = E[\,Y(1)-Y(0)\mid D=1\,] \), averaged over those who actually took the treatment.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">ATU &mdash; effect on the untreated</div><div class="definition-text">\( \text{ATU} = E[\,Y(1)-Y(0)\mid D=0\,] \), the effect the non-participants <em>would</em> have had.</div></div></div>
<p>&ldquo;Did MGNREGA help the people who used it?&rdquo; is an ATT question. &ldquo;Should we extend the guarantee to new districts?&rdquo; is closer to ATU or ATE. A design that recovers the ATT says little about the second question if effects differ across groups. Naming the estimand before choosing a method is the discipline this course keeps.</p>

<h3>Why the Naive Comparison Misleads</h3>
<p>Compare the average outcome of the treated with that of the untreated and call the gap the effect. Adding and subtracting \(E[Y(0)\mid D=1]\) &mdash; the counterfactual mean for the treated &mdash; following Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009, ch. 2):</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \underbrace{E[Y\mid D=1]-E[Y\mid D=0]}_{\text{naive gap}} \;=\; \underbrace{E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid D=1]}_{\text{ATT}} \;+\; \underbrace{E[Y(0)\mid D=1]-E[Y(0)\mid D=0]}_{\text{selection bias}} \]</div><div class="equation-caption">The braced selection-bias term asks: how would the treated have done untreated, compared with how the untreated actually did? It is rarely zero, and you cannot sign it in advance.</div></div>

<div class="dag-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 460 150" role="img" aria-label="Directed acyclic graph: confounder U points to treatment D and outcome Y; D points to Y" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><defs><marker id="ah1" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/></marker></defs><circle cx="60" cy="110" r="22" stroke-width="2"/><text x="60" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">D</text><circle cx="400" cy="110" r="22" stroke-width="2"/><text x="400" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Y</text><circle cx="230" cy="30" r="22" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="230" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">U</text><line x1="84" y1="110" x2="374" y2="110" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah1)"/><line x1="212" y1="48" x2="80" y2="92" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah1)"/><line x1="248" y1="48" x2="380" y2="92" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah1)"/></svg><div class="dag-caption">Selection as confounding. An unobserved <em>U</em> (here, how poor a household is) drives both whether it works on a site (<em>D</em>) and its consumption (<em>Y</em>). The dashed node is unobserved. Module 3 makes this language exact.</div></div>

<h3>SUTVA: The Assumption Hiding in the Notation</h3>
<p>Writing \(Y_i(1)\) and \(Y_i(0)\) already assumes that unit <em>i</em>'s outcome depends only on its <em>own</em> treatment, and that &ldquo;treatment&rdquo; means one well-defined thing. That is the Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption (Rubin 1980).</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>No interference.</strong> Imbert &amp; Papp (2015) show MGNREGA raised private rural wages even for households that never worked a single day on a site. The treatment spilled through the labour market onto the &ldquo;untreated&rdquo;, contaminating any control group built from them.</p><p><strong>No hidden variations of treatment.</strong> A scheme paid promptly is not the same treatment as one paid late with leakage. If take-up bundles different doses, \(\tau_i\) is not well defined.</p></div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2"/></svg> Worked example &mdash; selection can flip the sign</div>
<p>Suppose the true ATT of an MGNREGA spell is <strong>+400</strong> rupees of monthly consumption. But participants are poorer: even untreated they would have consumed <strong>600</strong> less than non-participants. The naive gap mixes the two.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value (&#8377;/month)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>True effect on the treated (ATT)</td><td>+400</td></tr><tr><td>Selection bias \(\;E[Y(0)\mid D{=}1]-E[Y(0)\mid D{=}0]\)</td><td>&minus;600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Naive participant &minus; non-participant gap</strong></td><td><strong>&minus;200</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The naive number says the programme <em>hurt</em> people. It helped. Selection ran the other way and swamped the effect &mdash; the default whenever take-up is voluntary and correlated with the outcome.</p></div>

<h3>Seeing It in Code</h3>
<p>Simulate a programme where poorer households are likelier to take up, then watch the naive estimate diverge from the true ATT of 400.</p>
<pre><code>## R — selection bias in a simulated programme
set.seed(2026)
n        &lt;- 20000
baseline &lt;- rnorm(n, 3000, 800)               # latent consumption
p_take   &lt;- plogis(-(baseline - 3000) / 400)  # poorer =&gt; likelier to work
D        &lt;- rbinom(n, 1, p_take)
Y        &lt;- baseline + 400 * D                 # true ATT = 400
naive    &lt;- mean(Y[D == 1]) - mean(Y[D == 0])
cat(sprintf("Naive gap: %.0f   |   True ATT: 400\n", naive))
# Naive gap is strongly negative — selection, not the programme.</code></pre>
<pre><code>* Stata — same experiment
set seed 2026
set obs 20000
gen baseline = rnormal(3000, 800)
gen p_take   = invlogit(-(baseline - 3000)/400)
gen D        = runiform() &lt; p_take
gen Y        = baseline + 400*D          // true ATT = 400
mean Y if D==1
mean Y if D==0                            // compare the two means</code></pre>

<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Common pitfalls.</strong> (1) Reporting a before-vs-after change as &ldquo;impact&rdquo; &mdash; that compares \(Y\) over time, not \(Y(1)\) against \(Y(0)\). (2) Assuming a large sample removes bias; it only sharpens a biased number. (3) Quoting an ATT and answering an ATE question, or the reverse.</p></div></div>

<div class="problem-set"><div class="problem-set-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-3 7h3m-3 4h3m-6-4h.01M9 16h.01"/></svg> Practice &mdash; work these before Module 2</div>
<ol>
<li>A district reports that anganwadi children who attended a nutrition camp grew 0.3&nbsp;cm more last year than those who did not. Write this claim in potential-outcomes notation and state the one quantity you would need but cannot observe.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">The claim compares observed \(E[Y\mid D{=}1]\) and \(E[Y\mid D{=}0]\). The effect on attenders is \(E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid D{=}1]\) (the ATT). The missing quantity is \(E[Y(0)\mid D{=}1]\): how the attenders <em>would</em> have grown had they not attended. Parents who send children to camps differ, so non-attenders are a poor stand-in for it.</div></details></li>
<li>In the decomposition above, give one realistic story for an MGNREGA evaluation in which selection bias is <em>positive</em> (the naive gap overstates the ATT).
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">If sites open mainly in better-connected villages, participants may have <em>higher</em> untreated consumption than non-participants, so \(E[Y(0)\mid D{=}1] &gt; E[Y(0)\mid D{=}0]\) and the bias is positive. The sign of selection is an empirical question about <em>who</em> takes up, not a constant.</div></details></li>
<li>Give a concrete SUTVA violation for a sanitation programme that builds toilets in half the households of a village, and say which potential outcome it makes ill-defined.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">Disease transmission is communal: a treated household's diarrhoea outcome depends on whether <em>neighbours</em> also got toilets (a spillover / herd effect). Then \(Y_i(0)\) is not unique &mdash; it depends on others' treatment &mdash; so the &ldquo;no interference&rdquo; part of SUTVA fails and the unit-level effect is ill-defined.</div></details></li>
</ol></div>

<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">When a funder asks &ldquo;did it work?&rdquo;, they almost always mean the ATT &mdash; but they write indicators as if they mean the ATE. In coaching we pin the estimand to the decision the evidence has to serve, before a single indicator is drafted. It saves a great deal of arguing at the review meeting.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>

<div class="source-citation">Sources: Holland (1986), <em>JASA</em> 81(396); Splawa-Neyman (1923/1990), <em>Statistical Science</em> 5(4); Rubin (1974), <em>J. Educ. Psychol.</em> 66(5); Rubin (1980), <em>JASA</em> 75; Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009), ch. 2; Imbert &amp; Papp (2015), <em>AEJ: Applied</em> 7(2).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01621459.1986.10478354" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Holland, P. (1986). &ldquo;Statistics and Causal Inference.&rdquo; <em>JASA</em> 81(396).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691120355/mostly-harmless-econometrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009). <em>Mostly Harmless Econometrics</em>, ch. 2.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/app.20130401" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Imbert &amp; Papp (2015). &ldquo;Labor Market Effects of Social Programs.&rdquo; <em>AEJ: Applied</em> 7(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of the counterfactual logic before moving to selection bias in Module 2.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The fundamental problem of causal inference (Holland 1986) is that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1a" value="a"><label for="m1q1a">Samples are usually too small to detect real effects.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1b" value="b"><label for="m1q1b">Treatment is rarely randomly assigned.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1c" value="c"><label for="m1q1c">For any unit we observe only one potential outcome, so the individual effect is never seen.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1d" value="d"><label for="m1q1d">Outcomes are measured with error.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A rural employment guarantee raises wages even for non-participants because the local labour market tightens. Which assumption is violated? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2a" value="a"><label for="m1q2a">The estimand is the ATT not the ATE.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2b" value="b"><label for="m1q2b">SUTVA, the no-interference half.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2c" value="c"><label for="m1q2c">Random assignment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2d" value="d"><label for="m1q2d">Common support.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Participants are poorer and would have consumed less even untreated. The true ATT is positive. The naive gap will be: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3a" value="a"><label for="m1q3a">Biased downward, possibly negative, because selection bias is negative.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3b" value="b"><label for="m1q3b">Unbiased, since both groups use the same outcome.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3c" value="c"><label for="m1q3c">Biased upward, since participants gain.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3d" value="d"><label for="m1q3d">Equal to the ATU.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Think of one programme you have worked on where take-up was voluntary. Who selected in, and would they have done better or worse than non-participants even with no programme? State whether that makes the naive comparison an over- or under-estimate.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  true),
('causal', 2, 'Module 2: Selection Bias and the Identifying Assumption',
  'When the selection-bias term is zero, the difference between selection on observables and unobservables, and why controlling for things is not the same as identification.',
  $html$<h2>Module 2: Selection Bias and the Identifying Assumption</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Module 1 showed that the naive comparison equals the effect <em>plus</em> selection bias. Every method in this course is a strategy for setting that bias term to zero. The first strategy is the most demanding: assume that, once you control for the right observed characteristics, who got treated is as good as random. This module states that assumption exactly, shows what it buys, and shows why it is so hard to defend.</p></div>

<h3>The Identifying Assumption</h3>
<p>Adjustment methods rest on one condition, written several ways &mdash; conditional independence, unconfoundedness, ignorability, or &ldquo;selection on observables&rdquo;. All say the same thing: within cells defined by observed covariates \(X\), treatment is unrelated to the potential outcomes.</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \{\,Y(0),\,Y(1)\,\} \;\perp\; D \;\mid\; X \]</div><div class="equation-caption">Conditional Independence Assumption (CIA). Among units with the same \(X\), the treated and untreated are comparable: their untreated outcomes would have had the same distribution.</div></div>
<p>A second, easily forgotten, condition is needed for the comparison to exist at all:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ 0 \;<\; \Pr(D=1 \mid X) \;<\; 1 \quad\text{for all }X \]</div><div class="equation-caption">Overlap (common support). At every value of \(X\) you must see both treated and untreated units. Without it there is no one to compare, and the model silently extrapolates.</div></div>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> What it buys you</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Identification of the ATT</div><div class="definition-text">Under CIA + overlap, \( \text{ATT} = E_X\!\big[\,E[Y\mid D{=}1,X] - E[Y\mid D{=}0,X]\,\big] \). Match like with like on \(X\), difference within cells, then average.</div></div></div>

<h3>Selection on Observables vs Unobservables</h3>
<p>The DAG makes the bet visible. If the only thing driving both treatment and outcome is observed (\(X\)), conditioning on it closes the backdoor path and the bias disappears. If anything <em>unobserved</em> (\(U\)) does so, no amount of controlling for \(X\) helps.</p>
<div class="dag-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 470 170" role="img" aria-label="Two DAGs: conditioning on observed X identifies the effect; an unobserved U does not" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><defs><marker id="ah2" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/></marker></defs><circle cx="45" cy="120" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="45" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">D</text><circle cx="205" cy="120" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="205" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Y</text><rect x="105" y="20" width="40" height="34" rx="4" stroke-width="2"/><text x="125" y="42" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">X</text><line x1="65" y1="120" x2="183" y2="120" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><line x1="110" y1="50" x2="55" y2="102" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><line x1="140" y1="50" x2="196" y2="102" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><text x="125" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">X observed → adjust → identified</text><circle cx="285" cy="120" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="285" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">D</text><circle cx="445" cy="120" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="445" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Y</text><circle cx="365" cy="32" r="20" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="365" y="37" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">U</text><line x1="305" y1="120" x2="423" y2="120" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><line x1="349" y1="48" x2="295" y2="102" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><line x1="381" y1="48" x2="436" y2="102" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><text x="365" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">U unobserved → bias remains</text></svg><div class="dag-caption">Left: the confounder is observed, so adjustment works. Right: the confounder is unobserved (motivation, ability, household connections), so &ldquo;controlling for what we have&rdquo; leaves bias behind.</div></div>

<h3>How Big Is the Bias? The Omitted-Variable Formula</h3>
<p>Regress \(Y\) on \(D\) leaving out a confounder \(U\). The short-regression coefficient is the long-regression coefficient plus a bias term:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \widehat{\beta}_{\text{short}} \;\xrightarrow{p}\; \beta_{\text{true}} \;+\; \underbrace{\delta_{U}\,\cdot\,\gamma_{U\to D}}_{\text{omitted-variable bias}} \]</div><div class="equation-caption">Bias = (effect of \(U\) on \(Y\)) &times; (association of \(U\) with \(D\)). It vanishes only if \(U\) does not affect the outcome, or is unrelated to treatment. &ldquo;Controlling for more&rdquo; helps only to the extent the added variable stands in for \(U\).</div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2"/></svg> Worked example &mdash; a skilling programme</div>
<p>A youth skilling programme reports a &#8377;1,800/month earnings premium for graduates over non-graduates. Graduates are younger, better-educated, and urban. Adjusting for these observed differences shrinks the estimate; what remains still rests on assuming nothing <em>unobserved</em> (drive, family contacts) separates the groups.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Estimated premium (&#8377;/month)</th><th>Identifying bet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Raw difference in means</td><td>+1,800</td><td>none &mdash; pure selection</td></tr><tr><td>+ age, education, urban, sector</td><td>+700</td><td>CIA on these covariates</td></tr><tr><td>+ baseline earnings (pre-programme)</td><td>+450</td><td>CIA incl. prior trajectory</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The estimate falls by three-quarters as controls accumulate. The honest report states the last number <em>and</em> the assumption it requires &mdash; not the first.</p></div>

<h3>In Code</h3>
<pre><code>## R — adjustment by regression, and a balance check
m_raw  &lt;- lm(earnings ~ trained, data = d)
m_adj  &lt;- lm(earnings ~ trained + age + educ + urban + base_earn, data = d)
summary(m_adj)$coef["trained", ]
## overlap: propensity distribution should overlap across groups
ps &lt;- glm(trained ~ age + educ + urban + base_earn, family = binomial, data = d)$fitted
tapply(ps, d$trained, summary)   # check common support before trusting the estimate</code></pre>
<pre><code>* Stata — same adjustment and an overlap check
regress earnings i.trained
regress earnings i.trained age educ i.urban base_earn
logit trained age educ i.urban base_earn
predict ps, pr
by trained, sort: summarize ps      // do the supports overlap?</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Pitfalls.</strong> CIA is an <em>untestable</em> assumption &mdash; balance on observables cannot prove balance on unobservables. Never control for a variable affected <em>by</em> treatment (a mediator or post-treatment control); Module 3 shows it can create bias rather than remove it. And a precise estimate off no overlap is precise extrapolation, not evidence.</p></div></div>

<div class="problem-set"><div class="problem-set-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-3 7h3m-3 4h3m-6-4h.01M9 16h.01"/></svg> Practice</div>
<ol>
<li>State the CIA in words for a programme that gives interest-free loans to women who <em>apply</em>. Name one plausible unobserved \(U\) that would break it.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">CIA: among applicants with the same observed \(X\) (age, assets, prior income), getting the loan is unrelated to what their business outcomes <em>would</em> have been either way. A natural breaker is entrepreneurial drive or risk tolerance &mdash; unobserved, it raises both the chance of applying/qualifying and later profits, so it sits in \(U\) and the adjusted estimate stays biased upward.</div></details></li>
<li>Using the omitted-variable formula, explain why adding household wealth as a control could either raise or lower the estimated treatment effect.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">The bias term is \(\delta_U\gamma_{U\to D}\). If wealthier households are likelier to take up (\(\gamma&gt;0\)) and wealth raises the outcome (\(\delta&gt;0\)), omitting wealth biases the estimate <em>up</em>, so adding it lowers the coefficient. If the poor are targeted (\(\gamma&lt;0\)) while wealth still raises the outcome, the omitted bias is negative and adding wealth <em>raises</em> the coefficient. Sign depends on both directions.</div></details></li>
<li>Why is overlap as important as CIA? Sketch what an estimator does when \(\Pr(D{=}1\mid X)\approx 1\) for some \(X\).
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">With no untreated units at that \(X\), there is no empirical counterfactual; a regression supplies one by extrapolating its functional form into a region with no data. The &ldquo;estimate&rdquo; there is an artefact of the model, not a comparison. Good practice trims to the region of common support and reports the estimand for that region.</div></details></li>
</ol></div>

<div class="source-citation">Sources: Rosenbaum &amp; Rubin (1983), <em>Biometrika</em> 70(1); Imbens (2004), <em>Review of Economics and Statistics</em> 86(1); Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009), ch. 3; Cunningham (2021), <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em>, ch. 5.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="https://academic.oup.com/biomet/article/70/1/41/240879" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Rosenbaum &amp; Rubin (1983). &ldquo;The Central Role of the Propensity Score&hellip;&rdquo; <em>Biometrika</em> 70(1).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://mixtape.scunning.com/05-matching_and_subclassification" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Cunningham (2021). <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em>, ch. 5.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Make sure you can separate the two kinds of selection before the graphs in Module 3.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The conditional independence assumption (CIA) states that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1a" value="a"><label for="m2q1a">Treatment is independent of all covariates.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1b" value="b"><label for="m2q1b">Potential outcomes are independent of treatment once we condition on X.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1c" value="c"><label for="m2q1c">The outcome is independent of the covariates.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1d" value="d"><label for="m2q1d">The sample is independent across units.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Adding controls to a regression can remove selection bias only when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2a" value="a"><label for="m2q2a">The sample is large enough.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2b" value="b"><label for="m2q2b">The outcome is continuous.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2c" value="c"><label for="m2q2c">Selection is on observables that are in the data.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2d" value="d"><label for="m2q2d">The treatment is binary.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> LaLonde (1986) is cited to show that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3a" value="a"><label for="m2q3a">Observational estimators often miss the experimental benchmark by wide, unpredictable margins.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3b" value="b"><label for="m2q3b">RCTs are infeasible in poor countries.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3c" value="c"><label for="m2q3c">Propensity scores always work.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3d" value="d"><label for="m2q3d">Selection bias is usually positive.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take a non-experimental result you have relied on. Name the single most important unobserved driver of both participation and the outcome. Could you measure it? If not, what does that do to the estimate?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 3, 'Module 3: Causal Graphs: Confounders, Colliders, and Bad Controls',
  'Directed acyclic graphs as the tool that decides which variables to adjust for, the backdoor criterion, and the controls that make bias worse.',
  $html$<h2>Module 3: Causal Graphs: Confounders, Colliders, and Bad Controls</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Module 2 said &ldquo;control for the right covariates.&rdquo; Which ones are right? A directed acyclic graph (DAG) answers that visually and without algebra. It also delivers the course's most counter-intuitive lesson: some perfectly sensible-looking controls <em>create</em> bias. Getting the control set wrong is the most common avoidable error in applied evaluation.</p></div>

<h3>Reading a DAG</h3>
<p>A DAG is a set of variables (nodes) and arrows (direct causal effects). A <em>path</em> is any route between two nodes ignoring arrow direction. Association flows along a path unless something blocks it. Pearl's rules of <em>d-separation</em> tell you when a path is open:</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Three building blocks</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Chain &nbsp;\(A \to B \to C\)</div><div class="definition-text">\(B\) is a mediator. The path is open; conditioning on \(B\) <em>blocks</em> it (and removes part of the effect you may want).</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Fork &nbsp;\(A \leftarrow B \to C\)</div><div class="definition-text">\(B\) is a common cause (confounder). The path is open; conditioning on \(B\) <em>blocks</em> it &mdash; this is the good kind of control.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Collider &nbsp;\(A \to B \leftarrow C\)</div><div class="definition-text">\(B\) is a common effect. The path is <em>blocked by default</em>; conditioning on \(B\) (or its descendant) <em>opens</em> it, creating spurious association.</div></div></div>

<h3>The Backdoor Criterion</h3>
<p>To identify the effect of \(D\) on \(Y\), block every <em>backdoor</em> path (one that starts with an arrow <em>into</em> \(D\)) without opening any collider. A covariate set \(Z\) that does this satisfies the backdoor criterion, and adjusting for \(Z\) recovers the causal effect.</p>
<div class="dag-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 180" role="img" aria-label="DAG with a confounder to adjust for and a collider not to adjust for" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><defs><marker id="ah3" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/></marker></defs><circle cx="60" cy="130" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="60" y="135" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">D</text><circle cx="300" cy="130" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="300" y="135" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Y</text><circle cx="180" cy="35" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="180" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">W</text><circle cx="180" cy="150" r="20" stroke-width="2"/><text x="180" y="155" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">C</text><line x1="80" y1="130" x2="278" y2="130" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah3)"/><line x1="165" y1="50" x2="73" y2="113" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah3)"/><line x1="195" y1="50" x2="288" y2="113" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah3)"/><line x1="78" y1="138" x2="162" y2="148" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah3)"/><line x1="282" y1="138" x2="198" y2="148" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ah3)"/><text x="395" y="60" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">W: confounder — ADJUST</text><text x="395" y="150" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">C: collider — DO NOT adjust</text></svg><div class="dag-caption">\(W\) (a fork: \(D \leftarrow W \to Y\)) opens a backdoor &mdash; adjust for it. \(C\) (a collider: \(D \to C \leftarrow Y\)) is closed &mdash; adjusting for it <em>opens</em> a spurious path and biases the estimate.</div></div>

<h3>Bad Controls: When &ldquo;More Controls&rdquo; Is Wrong</h3>
<p>Two controls look helpful but are not. <strong>Colliders</strong>: conditioning on a common effect of \(D\) and \(Y\) induces selection bias (the classic &ldquo;why are talented athletes less academic <em>in the selected sample</em>&rdquo; illusion). <strong>Mediators</strong>: conditioning on a variable <em>caused</em> by \(D\) removes the very effect you are measuring (over-control bias). A field example: studying whether a cash transfer (\(D\)) raises child nutrition (\(Y\)) and &ldquo;controlling for&rdquo; household food spending &mdash; which the transfer itself changes &mdash; throws away the mechanism.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The rule.</strong> Only adjust for variables determined <em>before</em> treatment that sit on a backdoor path. Never adjust for post-treatment variables. &ldquo;Kitchen-sink&rdquo; regressions that dump in every available column routinely include colliders and mediators &mdash; more controls is not safer.</p></div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2"/></svg> Worked example &mdash; the same coefficient, three control sets</div>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Control set for effect of training \(D\) on earnings \(Y\)</th><th>Status</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pre-programme: age, schooling, district</td><td>good (confounders)</td><td>closes backdoors &rarr; identifies</td></tr><tr><td>Add: current job sector (post-training)</td><td>mediator</td><td>removes part of the effect</td></tr><tr><td>Add: &ldquo;selected into a follow-up survey&rdquo;</td><td>collider</td><td>opens spurious path &rarr; bias</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Same data, same \(D\) and \(Y\); the estimate is trustworthy only with the first set. The choice is dictated by the graph, not by which controls improve fit.</p></div>

<h3>In Code: Let the Graph Choose the Controls</h3>
<pre><code>## R — encode the DAG and ask which set identifies the effect
library(dagitty)
g &lt;- dagitty('dag {
  D -&gt; Y
  W -&gt; D   W -&gt; Y         // confounder
  D -&gt; C   Y -&gt; C         // collider
}')
adjustmentSets(g, exposure = "D", outcome = "Y")   # returns { W } — not C
isCollider(g, "D", "C", "Y")                        # TRUE</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Draw the DAG <em>before</em> you open the data. Pre-registering the graph forces the assumptions into the open and decides the control set in advance &mdash; the opposite of fishing for the specification that gives the prettiest coefficient.</p></div></div>

<div class="problem-set"><div class="problem-set-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-3 7h3m-3 4h3m-6-4h.01M9 16h.01"/></svg> Practice</div>
<ol>
<li>You study whether an NGO's presence (\(D\)) reduces child marriage (\(Y\)). Districts get the NGO partly because of poverty (\(W\)), which also affects marriage. Draw the DAG and state the adjustment set.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">\(D \leftarrow W \to Y\) with \(D \to Y\). \(W\) is a fork on the backdoor path \(D \leftarrow W \to Y\); adjust for \(W\) (poverty) and the effect is identified, assuming no other open backdoor. Nothing post-treatment should enter.</div></details></li>
<li>A study of scholarship (\(D\)) on graduation (\(Y\)) controls for &ldquo;enrolled in year 3.&rdquo; Why might this be a bad control?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">Staying enrolled to year 3 is plausibly <em>caused</em> by the scholarship and also predicts graduation &mdash; a mediator on the \(D \to \text{enrolment} \to Y\) chain. Conditioning on it removes the part of the effect that runs through retention, understating impact (over-control bias).</div></details></li>
<li>Explain the &ldquo;collider&rdquo; intuition behind the finding that, <em>among hospital patients</em>, two unrelated diseases appear negatively correlated.
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">Admission (the collider) is caused by either disease being severe. Conditioning on &ldquo;admitted&rdquo; opens the path between the two diseases: among the admitted, a patient without one disease is likelier to have the other, manufacturing a negative association that does not exist in the population. Sample selection <em>is</em> collider conditioning.</div></details></li>
</ol></div>

<div class="source-citation">Sources: Pearl (2009), <em>Causality</em>, 2nd ed.; Cinelli, Forney &amp; Pearl (2022), &ldquo;A Crash Course in Good and Bad Controls,&rdquo; <em>Sociological Methods &amp; Research</em>; Hernán &amp; Robins (2020), <em>Causal Inference: What If</em>, ch. 6&ndash;7; Elwert &amp; Winship (2014), <em>Annual Review of Sociology</em> 40.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00491241221099552" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Cinelli, Forney &amp; Pearl (2022). &ldquo;A Crash Course in Good and Bad Controls.&rdquo;</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/miguel-hernan/causal-inference-book/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Hernán &amp; Robins (2020). <em>Causal Inference: What If</em>, ch. 6&ndash;7.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm you can spot a confounder, a mediator, and a collider before the estimator modules.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> In the graph X &rarr; M &rarr; Y, the variable M is a: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1a" value="a"><label for="m3q1a">Confounder you must control for.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1b" value="b"><label for="m3q1b">Mediator that carries the effect; controlling for it removes part of the effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1c" value="c"><label for="m3q1c">Collider that is safe to ignore.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1d" value="d"><label for="m3q1d">Instrument.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Conditioning on a collider: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2a" value="a"><label for="m3q2a">Always reduces bias.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2b" value="b"><label for="m3q2b">Has no effect on the estimate.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2c" value="c"><label for="m3q2c">Opens a spurious path and can create bias that was not there.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2d" value="d"><label for="m3q2d">Is required by the backdoor criterion.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> You estimate returns to training and control for current job sector, which is caused by training. This is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3a" value="a"><label for="m3q3a">A bad control that removes part of the causal effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3b" value="b"><label for="m3q3b">Always correct, since more controls reduce bias.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3c" value="c"><label for="m3q3c">A valid instrument.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3d" value="d"><label for="m3q3d">Required for overlap.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Sketch a quick DAG for an intervention you know, including at least one unobserved confounder as a dashed node. Identify one variable that would be a bad control, and say why.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 4, 'Module 4: Randomisation and the RCT',
  'Why random assignment zeroes selection bias, the ITT and LATE estimands, and the four threats (non-compliance, attrition, spillovers, external validity) that break trials in the field.',
  $html$<h2>Module 4: Randomisation and the RCT</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">The first three modules built a problem and an assumption that is hard to defend. Randomisation defends it by construction: if a coin decides who is treated, the treated and untreated are comparable in everything &mdash; observed and unobserved alike. That is the whole power of the randomised controlled trial, and also the source of its limits.</p></div>

<h3>Why Randomisation Works</h3>
<p>Assign \(D\) by a fair lottery and treatment is, by design, independent of the potential outcomes &mdash; not just within \(X\)-cells, but unconditionally:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \{\,Y(0),\,Y(1)\,\} \;\perp\; D \]</div><div class="equation-caption">The selection-bias term from Module 1 is zero in expectation, because \(E[Y(0)\mid D{=}1]=E[Y(0)\mid D{=}0]\). The control group's average outcome <em>is</em> the treated group's missing counterfactual.</div></div>
<p>It follows that the simplest possible estimator is unbiased for the ATE:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \widehat{\text{ATE}} \;=\; \bar{Y}_{\,\text{treated}} - \bar{Y}_{\,\text{control}} \]</div><div class="equation-caption">A difference in means. No modelling of the outcome, no assumption about functional form, no list of controls to defend. This is why the RCT is the reference design.</div></div>

<h3>What Randomisation Does and Does Not Buy</h3>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Design choices that matter</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Balance, not proof</div><div class="definition-text">Randomisation balances groups <em>in expectation</em>. Report a baseline balance table; a few imbalances by chance are normal. Covariates can be added to the regression to raise <em>precision</em>, but they are not needed to remove bias.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Stratification / blocking</div><div class="definition-text">Randomise within strata (e.g. by district and baseline score) to guarantee balance on the variables that matter most and to cut variance.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Clustering</div><div class="definition-text">If you randomise whole villages or schools, units within a cluster are correlated. Use cluster-robust standard errors and power the study on the number of <em>clusters</em>, not individuals.</div></div></div>

<h3>Intention-to-Treat vs the Treated</h3>
<p>People assigned to treatment do not always take it. Two estimands diverge:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \text{LATE} \;=\; \frac{\text{ITT effect on the outcome}}{\text{effect of assignment on take-up}} \;=\; \frac{\bar{Y}_{Z=1}-\bar{Y}_{Z=0}}{\bar{D}_{Z=1}-\bar{D}_{Z=0}} \]</div><div class="equation-caption">The intention-to-treat (ITT) compares <em>as assigned</em> &mdash; the policy-relevant number when take-up is itself part of the programme. Dividing by the take-up gap rescales it to the effect on compliers (the LATE of Module 6). Never compare only those who actually complied: that re-introduces selection.</div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2"/></svg> Worked example &mdash; incentives for immunisation (Rajasthan)</div>
<p>Banerjee, Duflo, Glennerster &amp; Kothari (2010) randomised villages in Udaipur to reliable monthly immunisation camps, with or without a small incentive (a kilo of lentils per shot). Full immunisation of children rose dramatically &mdash; and the incentive arm did better despite &ldquo;paying&rdquo; people, because it offset the cost of repeat visits.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Full immunisation rate</th><th>Difference vs control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Control (status quo)</td><td>&asymp; 6%</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><td>Reliable camp only</td><td>&asymp; 18%</td><td>+12 pp</td></tr><tr><td>Reliable camp + incentive</td><td>&asymp; 39%</td><td>+33 pp</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Each number is a difference in means against the randomised control &mdash; no modelling required &mdash; and the design answers a policy question (does a small incentive beat exhortation?) that no observational comparison could settle credibly.</p></div>

<h3>In Code</h3>
<pre><code>## R — difference in means, then add strata FE + clustered SE for precision
library(estimatr)
fit &lt;- lm_robust(immunised ~ incentive,
                 fixed_effects = ~ strata,
                 clusters = village, data = d)
tidy(fit)["incentive", c("estimate","std.error","conf.low","conf.high")]
# Minimum detectable effect at 80% power for a two-arm cluster design:
# power.t.test() / pwr, or the 'DeclareDesign' package to simulate it.</code></pre>
<pre><code>* Stata — ITT with stratum fixed effects and village-clustered SEs
regress immunised i.incentive i.strata, vce(cluster village)
* take-up of the offer (first stage), for rescaling ITT to LATE
regress attended i.incentive i.strata, vce(cluster village)</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Threats even in an RCT.</strong> Differential <em>attrition</em> (treatment changes who you can find at endline) re-opens selection. <em>Spillovers</em> from treated to control violate SUTVA. And an RCT identifies the effect <em>for the studied population</em> &mdash; external validity (will it travel?) is a separate, judgement-heavy question taken up in Module 11.</p></div></div>

<div class="problem-set"><div class="problem-set-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-3 7h3m-3 4h3m-6-4h.01M9 16h.01"/></svg> Practice</div>
<ol>
<li>A reviewer says &ldquo;your treatment and control differ on baseline literacy, so the randomisation failed.&rdquo; When is this objection wrong, and how do you answer it?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">With many baseline variables, a few significant differences are expected by chance even under valid randomisation. The objection is wrong if the imbalance is within what chance predicts and the assignment was genuinely random. Answer by reporting the full balance table, noting the expected number of &ldquo;significant&rdquo; differences, and showing the estimate is robust to controlling for the imbalanced covariate (which should barely move it).</div></details></li>
<li>Only 60% of those offered a job-training place attend; the rest do not. You find attendees out-earn non-attendees by &#8377;2,000. Why is this not the causal effect, and what should you report?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">Comparing attendees with non-attendees compares self-selected groups &mdash; pure selection. Report the ITT (offered vs not offered, regardless of attendance). To recover the effect on those induced to attend, rescale: \(\text{LATE} = \text{ITT}/0.60\) (assuming no always-/never-taker contamination), exactly the IV logic of Module 6.</div></details></li>
<li>You randomise 40 villages (20/20) rather than 4,000 individuals. Why might the study be underpowered even with 4,000 people in it?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">Outcomes within a village are correlated (intra-cluster correlation \(\rho\)). Effective sample size is closer to the number of clusters than the number of people; the design effect \(1+(m-1)\rho\) inflates the variance. With only 40 clusters and non-trivial \(\rho\), the minimum detectable effect can be large despite 4,000 individuals. Power on clusters.</div></details></li>
</ol></div>

<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana</div><div class="coach-message">The hardest part of a real trial is rarely the statistics &mdash; it is keeping the control group truly untreated and tracking everyone to endline. In design clinics we spend most of our time on take-up, attrition and spillover logistics, because that is where good RCTs are won or lost.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book a design clinic</a></div></div></div>

<div class="source-citation">Sources: Banerjee, Duflo, Glennerster &amp; Kothari (2010), <em>BMJ</em> 340:c2220; Duflo, Glennerster &amp; Kremer (2007), &ldquo;Using Randomization in Development Economics,&rdquo; <em>Handbook of Development Economics</em> 4; Athey &amp; Imbens (2017), <em>JEP</em> 31(2); Gerber &amp; Green (2012), <em>Field Experiments</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/340/bmj.c2220" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Banerjee et al. (2010). &ldquo;Improving immunisation coverage&hellip;incentives.&rdquo; <em>BMJ</em> 340:c2220.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://www.povertyactionlab.org/resource/quick-guide-randomization-practice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>J-PAL. &ldquo;Quick Guide to Randomization in Practice.&rdquo;</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm the estimands and the field threats before moving to observational designs.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis compares groups by: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1a" value="a"><label for="m4q1a">Whether they actually received the treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1b" value="b"><label for="m4q1b">Their original random assignment, regardless of take-up.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1c" value="c"><label for="m4q1c">Their propensity score.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1d" value="d"><label for="m4q1d">Their baseline outcome.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Analysing an RCT by actual take-up rather than assignment: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2a" value="a"><label for="m4q2a">Reintroduces selection bias.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2b" value="b"><label for="m4q2b">Improves external validity.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2c" value="c"><label for="m4q2c">Is required to estimate the ITT.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2d" value="d"><label for="m4q2d">Removes attrition.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Randomisation primarily protects: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3a" value="a"><label for="m4q3a">External validity.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3b" value="b"><label for="m4q3b">Statistical power.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3c" value="c"><label for="m4q3c">Internal validity, by balancing observed and unobserved confounders.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3d" value="d"><label for="m4q3d">Measurement accuracy.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>For a programme you might evaluate, at what level would you randomise to avoid spillovers, and what would non-compliance realistically look like? Which estimand answers your funder’s question, ITT or LATE?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 5, 'Module 5: Matching and Propensity Scores',
  'Building a comparison group under selection on observables, propensity-score and coarsened exact matching, common support, and why balance is the real test.',
  $html$<h2>Module 5: Matching and Propensity Scores</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">When you cannot randomise but believe selection is on observables, matching builds a comparison group that looks like the treated on everything you measured. Its honesty lives entirely in that belief.</p></div>
<h3>The Idea</h3>
<p>For each treated unit, find untreated units with the same covariates and compare outcomes. With many covariates, exact matches run out. Rosenbaum and Rubin (1983) showed you can match on a single number instead, the propensity score, Pr(<em>D</em>=1 | <em>X</em>), and still balance <em>X</em>.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Methods and Diagnostics</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Propensity-score matching</div><div class="definition-text">Estimate the score, then match treated to control units with nearby scores (nearest neighbour, caliper, kernel).</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Coarsened exact matching</div><div class="definition-text">Iacus, King &amp; Porro (2012). Coarsen each covariate into bins, match exactly within bins. Transparent and bounds imbalance directly.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Common support</div><div class="definition-text">Drop treated units with no comparable control and vice versa. You can only compare where both groups exist.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Balance check</div><div class="definition-text">After matching, covariate means should be close across groups. Standardised differences under about 0.1 are the usual target. Balance, not the regression output, is the test of success.</div></div></div>
<p>A frequent application: comparing facilities that adopted a quality-certification programme with similar uncertified facilities, matched on size, caseload, location, and prior performance, then comparing outcomes. Done to lending-industry standards, a careful matched comparison can persuade a risk committee even when an RCT was never possible.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>Matching does not solve selection on unobservables.</strong> It makes selection on observables transparent and checkable. If the thing that drove participation is unmeasured, a perfectly balanced match is still biased. Dehejia and Wahba (2002) showed matching can recover experimental benchmarks, but only when the right covariates are observed.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Balance is the deliverable, not the coefficient.</strong> Report covariate balance before and after matching. A reviewer who sees only the treatment effect, and no balance table, should not believe the number.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">I treat matching as a descriptive comparison dressed in careful clothing, not as identification. It is often the most honest thing available, and I say so to the funder in those words. Overclaiming it as causal is how matched studies lose credibility.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: propensity-score matching with balance check
library(MatchIt)
m &lt;- matchit(treat ~ size + caseload + region + prior_perf,
             data = df, method = "nearest", caliper = 0.1)
summary(m)                       # check standardised mean differences
att &lt;- lm(outcome ~ treat, data = match.data(m))</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Rosenbaum &amp; Rubin (1983), <em>Biometrika</em> 70(1); Dehejia &amp; Wahba (2002), <em>REStat</em> 84(1); Iacus, King &amp; Porro (2012), <em>Political Analysis</em> 20(1).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Rosenbaum &amp; Rubin (1983). "The Central Role of the Propensity Score."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Iacus, King &amp; Porro (2012). "Causal Inference Without Balance Checking: Coarsened Exact Matching."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Stuart, E. (2010). "Matching Methods for Causal Inference: A Review." <em>Statistical Science</em> 25(1).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Check the assumptions and diagnostics before instruments arrive in Module 6.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The propensity score is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1a" value="a"><label for="m5q1a">The treatment effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1b" value="b"><label for="m5q1b">The probability of treatment given covariates, Pr(D=1|X).</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1c" value="c"><label for="m5q1c">The outcome under treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1d" value="d"><label for="m5q1d">A collider.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> After matching, the primary thing you report to justify the design is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2a" value="a"><label for="m5q2a">The R-squared.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2b" value="b"><label for="m5q2b">The number of observations.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2c" value="c"><label for="m5q2c">Covariate balance across treated and control.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2d" value="d"><label for="m5q2d">The p-value of the treatment.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Matching fails to remove bias when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3a" value="a"><label for="m5q3a">Selection is on unobserved variables not in X.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3b" value="b"><label for="m5q3b">The sample is large.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3c" value="c"><label for="m5q3c">Covariates are balanced.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3d" value="d"><label for="m5q3d">There is common support.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>For a comparison you cannot randomise, list the covariates you would match on. Then name the one unobserved variable that, if it drives both participation and outcome, would defeat the whole exercise.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 6, 'Module 6: Instrumental Variables',
  'Using clean variation in treatment, relevance and the untestable exclusion restriction, LATE and compliers, and why weak instruments mislead.',
  $html$<h2>Module 6: Instrumental Variables</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">When treatment is tangled with unobservables, an instrument finds a slice of variation in treatment that is clean, and uses only that slice. It is powerful and easy to misuse, in equal measure.</p></div>
<h3>What an Instrument Must Do</h3>
<p>An instrument <em>Z</em> shifts treatment but affects the outcome only through treatment. Rainfall shifts agricultural income; a school-construction programme shifts schooling; distance to a facility shifts take-up. Duflo (2001, <em>AER</em>) used the timing and intensity of Indonesia’s school-building programme as an instrument for schooling to estimate returns to education.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> The Two Conditions and the Estimand</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Relevance</div><div class="definition-text"><em>Z</em> genuinely moves <em>D</em>. Testable: the first-stage F-statistic should be well above 10 (Staiger &amp; Stock 1997).</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Exclusion</div><div class="definition-text"><em>Z</em> affects <em>Y</em> only through <em>D</em>, with no direct path and no back door. Not testable. It is an argument, and it is where most IV studies live or die.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">LATE</div><div class="definition-text">With heterogeneous effects, IV recovers the local average treatment effect, the effect on compliers, those whose treatment <em>Z</em> actually moved (Imbens &amp; Angrist 1994). It is not the ATE, and the complier group is usually not described.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>The exclusion restriction cannot be tested, only argued.</strong> Rainfall as an instrument for income assumes weather affects the outcome through income alone, not through health, conflict, or migration. State every other channel and why it is closed. If you cannot, the instrument is not credible.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Weak instruments lie quietly.</strong> Bound, Jaeger and Baker (1995) showed a weak first stage produces estimates biased toward the confounded OLS result, with confidence intervals that understate the damage. Always report the first-stage F. A large second-stage t-statistic on a weak instrument is not reassurance.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">When someone brings me an instrument, I ignore the second stage at first and interrogate two things: is the first stage strong, and can they close every alternative path from the instrument to the outcome. If the exclusion story takes more than two sentences and a lot of hedging, I do not believe it.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: two-stage least squares with first-stage diagnostics
library(ivreg)
m &lt;- ivreg(income ~ schooling + region | program_intensity + region, data = df)
summary(m, diagnostics = TRUE)   # inspect Weak instruments row</code></pre>
<pre><code>* Stata: 2SLS with first-stage F
ivregress 2sls income (schooling = program_intensity) i.region, first
estat firststage</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Imbens &amp; Angrist (1994), <em>Econometrica</em> 62(2); Duflo (2001), <em>AER</em> 91(4); Bound, Jaeger &amp; Baker (1995), <em>JASA</em> 90(430); Staiger &amp; Stock (1997).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009), <em>Mostly Harmless Econometrics</em>, ch. 4.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Imbens &amp; Angrist (1994). "Identification and Estimation of Local Average Treatment Effects."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Duflo, E. (2001). "Schooling and Labor Market Consequences of School Construction in Indonesia." <em>AER</em> 91(4).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Make sure you can state both conditions and name the estimand IV actually recovers.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The exclusion restriction requires that the instrument: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1a" value="a"><label for="m6q1a">Is correlated with the treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1b" value="b"><label for="m6q1b">Has a strong first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1c" value="c"><label for="m6q1c">Affects the outcome only through the treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1d" value="d"><label for="m6q1d">Is randomly assigned.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> With heterogeneous effects, IV recovers the: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2a" value="a"><label for="m6q2a">ATE over everyone.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2b" value="b"><label for="m6q2b">LATE, the effect on compliers whose treatment the instrument moved.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2c" value="c"><label for="m6q2c">ATT.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2d" value="d"><label for="m6q2d">ITT.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A first-stage F-statistic of 4 indicates: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3a" value="a"><label for="m6q3a">A weak instrument, biasing estimates toward OLS with misleadingly tight intervals.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3b" value="b"><label for="m6q3b">A strong instrument.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3c" value="c"><label for="m6q3c">A valid exclusion restriction.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3d" value="d"><label for="m6q3d">Perfect compliance.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Propose one instrument for a treatment you care about. Write the exclusion restriction in a single sentence, then list every other path from the instrument to the outcome and argue why each is closed.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 7, 'Module 7: Regression Discontinuity',
  'Identification at an eligibility cutoff, sharp and fuzzy designs, the local nature of the estimate, and the manipulation test that decides credibility.',
  $html$<h2>Module 7: Regression Discontinuity</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Many programmes assign benefits by a cutoff: a score, a population, a poverty line. Just above and just below the threshold, units are nearly identical except for the programme. That accident of administration is one of the most credible designs available.</p></div>
<h3>Identification at the Threshold</h3>
<p>A running variable determines treatment at a cutoff <em>c</em>. In a sharp design, everyone above <em>c</em> is treated and everyone below is not. Units just on either side are comparable, so the jump in the outcome at <em>c</em> is the causal effect, local to the threshold. Asher and Novosad (2020, <em>AER</em>) used India’s PMGSY rule, which prioritised rural roads for villages above a population threshold, as a regression discontinuity to estimate the effects of road construction.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Sharp, Fuzzy, and What You Estimate</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Sharp RD</div><div class="definition-text">Treatment jumps from 0 to 1 at the cutoff. The outcome jump is the effect at <em>c</em>.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Fuzzy RD</div><div class="definition-text">The probability of treatment jumps but not all the way. Scale the outcome jump by the treatment jump (an IV at the threshold).</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Local effect</div><div class="definition-text">RD identifies the effect for units near the cutoff only. A different threshold could give a different number.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>The design is only credible if units cannot precisely sort around the cutoff.</strong> If villages could manipulate their recorded population to clear the threshold, the units just above and below differ in ambition and administrative reach, and the comparison breaks. McCrary (2008) gives the density test for exactly this.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Two validity checks before you trust an RD.</strong> First, the McCrary density test: no bunching of the running variable at the cutoff. Second, covariate continuity: predetermined characteristics should not jump at <em>c</em>. Estimate with local linear regression and a data-driven bandwidth; report bias-corrected confidence intervals (Calonico, Cattaneo &amp; Titiunik 2014). A wide global polynomial is not a substitute.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">RD is my favourite design to commission because the assumption is visible. You can plot it. If the picture shows a clean jump at the cutoff and nothing jumping that should not, a non-technical board understands the evidence in one slide. That clarity is worth a great deal in a funding conversation.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: sharp RD with data-driven bandwidth and bias correction
library(rdrobust)
out &lt;- rdrobust(y = outcome, x = running_var, c = cutoff)
summary(out)
rddensity::rddensity(X = running_var, c = cutoff)   # McCrary-type test</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Imbens &amp; Lemieux (2008), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 142(2); Lee &amp; Lemieux (2010), <em>JEL</em> 48(2); Calonico, Cattaneo &amp; Titiunik (2014), <em>Econometrica</em> 82(6); Asher &amp; Novosad (2020), <em>AER</em> 110(3).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Lee &amp; Lemieux (2010). "Regression Discontinuity Designs in Economics." <em>JEL</em> 48(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Calonico, Cattaneo &amp; Titiunik (2014). "Robust Nonparametric Confidence Intervals for RD."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Asher &amp; Novosad (2020). "Rural Roads and Local Economic Development." <em>AER</em> 110(3).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm you can name the threat to an RD and the test for it.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Regression discontinuity identifies the treatment effect: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1a" value="a"><label for="m7q1a">On average over everyone.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1b" value="b"><label for="m7q1b">For compliers anywhere.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1c" value="c"><label for="m7q1c">Locally, for units near the cutoff.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1d" value="d"><label for="m7q1d">Only in randomised settings.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> The main threat to an RD design is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2a" value="a"><label for="m7q2a">Precise manipulation of the running variable around the cutoff.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2b" value="b"><label for="m7q2b">A large sample.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2c" value="c"><label for="m7q2c">A continuous outcome.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2d" value="d"><label for="m7q2d">A strong first stage.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> The McCrary density test checks for: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3a" value="a"><label for="m7q3a">Weak instruments.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3b" value="b"><label for="m7q3b">Bunching of the running variable at the cutoff.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3c" value="c"><label for="m7q3c">Parallel trends.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3d" value="d"><label for="m7q3d">Covariate balance after matching.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Name a programme assigned by a cutoff in your context. Could units manipulate the running variable to cross the threshold? What would you plot to convince a sceptic the design is clean?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 8, 'Module 8: Difference-in-Differences',
  'The canonical 2x2, parallel trends and pre-trend plots, and the staggered-adoption problem with the Goodman-Bacon decomposition and modern estimators.',
  $html$<h2>Module 8: Difference-in-Differences</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">When a policy arrives at different times or places, you can compare the change in a treated group to the change in a comparison group. The design rests on one assumption that has been a moving target in the literature since 2018.</p></div>
<h3>The Canonical 2x2</h3>
<p>Two groups, two periods. The treated group is exposed between periods; the comparison group is not. Subtract each group’s before-after change, then subtract the two differences. Whatever common shock hit both groups cancels. Card and Krueger (1994) made the design famous with the New Jersey minimum-wage study.</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">DiD = ( <em>Y</em><sub>treated,after</sub> &minus; <em>Y</em><sub>treated,before</sub> ) &minus; ( <em>Y</em><sub>control,after</sub> &minus; <em>Y</em><sub>control,before</sub> )</div><div class="equation-caption">The first difference removes fixed differences between units; the second removes the common time trend. What remains is the effect, if the trends would have moved together absent treatment.</div></div>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> The Identifying Assumption</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Parallel trends</div><div class="definition-text">Absent the treatment, treated and comparison groups would have followed the same trend. This is the whole ballgame, and it is not testable directly.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Pre-trends</div><div class="definition-text">Plot the groups before treatment. Divergence before the policy is evidence against parallel trends. Similar pre-trends are supportive, not proof (Roth 2022 warns against reading too much into pre-trend tests).</div></div></div>
<h3>The Staggered-Adoption Problem</h3>
<p>Most real policies in India arrive state by state, year by year. The reflex is a two-way fixed effects regression with unit and time dummies. Since 2018 we know this can fail badly. When treatment timing varies, two-way fixed effects implicitly uses already-treated units as controls for newly-treated ones, and can put negative weights on some effects. Goodman-Bacon (2021) decomposed exactly where the bias comes from.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>For staggered rollouts, do not trust a plain two-way fixed effects coefficient.</strong> Use an estimator built for the problem: Callaway and Sant’Anna (2021), Sun and Abraham (2021), or de Chaisemartin and D’Haultfœuille (2020). They estimate clean group-time effects and aggregate them with honest weights.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Report an event study, not a single number.</strong> Plot effects by time relative to treatment. Flat pre-period coefficients support parallel trends; a clear post-period jump shows the effect emerging. A lone DiD coefficient hides both the pre-trend and the dynamics.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">If a consultant hands me a staggered DiD with one number from a two-way fixed effects model and no event-study plot, I send it back. Since the Goodman-Bacon paper, that is no longer defensible, and funders’ technical reviewers know it.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: modern staggered DiD (Callaway & Sant\u2019Anna)
library(did)
att &lt;- att_gt(yname = "outcome", tname = "year", idname = "district",
              gname = "first_treat_year", data = df)
ggdid(aggte(att, type = "dynamic"))   # event-study plot</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Card &amp; Krueger (1994), <em>AER</em> 84(4); Goodman-Bacon (2021), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 225(2); Callaway &amp; Sant’Anna (2021), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 225(2); Sun &amp; Abraham (2021); de Chaisemartin &amp; D’Haultfœuille (2020), <em>AER</em> 110(9).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Goodman-Bacon (2021). "Difference-in-Differences with Variation in Treatment Timing."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Callaway &amp; Sant’Anna (2021). "Difference-in-Differences with Multiple Time Periods."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Roth, Sant’Anna, Bilinski &amp; Poe (2023). "What’s Trending in Difference-in-Differences?" <em>J. Econometrics</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm the assumption and the staggered-timing fix.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The identifying assumption of difference-in-differences is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1a" value="a"><label for="m8q1a">Random assignment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1b" value="b"><label for="m8q1b">Parallel trends: the groups would have moved together absent treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1c" value="c"><label for="m8q1c">A strong first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1d" value="d"><label for="m8q1d">No manipulation at a cutoff.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> With staggered treatment timing, a plain two-way fixed effects estimate can be biased because it: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2a" value="a"><label for="m8q2a">Uses already-treated units as controls and can apply negative weights.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2b" value="b"><label for="m8q2b">Has too few time periods.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2c" value="c"><label for="m8q2c">Ignores the running variable.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2d" value="d"><label for="m8q2d">Requires randomisation.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Similar pre-trends between groups are: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3a" value="a"><label for="m8q3a">Proof that parallel trends holds.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3b" value="b"><label for="m8q3b">Irrelevant to the design.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3c" value="c"><label for="m8q3c">Supportive evidence, not proof, of parallel trends.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3d" value="d"><label for="m8q3d">A sign of a weak instrument.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Think of a policy that rolled out across states or districts at different times. Which units would serve as the comparison early on, and why might a plain two-way fixed effects model mislead you here?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 9, 'Module 9: Synthetic Control',
  'Constructing a counterfactual from a weighted donor pool for single-unit interventions, the centrality of pre-period fit, and placebo-based inference.',
  $html$<h2>Module 9: Synthetic Control</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Sometimes only one unit is treated: one state passes a reform, one city runs a programme. With a single treated unit and no obvious comparison, synthetic control builds one from a weighted blend of untreated units that tracks the treated unit before the intervention.</p></div>
<h3>Building a Counterfactual from Donors</h3>
<p>Choose a donor pool of untreated units. Find weights on those donors so the weighted average matches the treated unit’s pre-intervention outcome path and predictors. After the intervention, the gap between the treated unit and its synthetic version is the estimated effect. Abadie and Gardeazabal (2003) introduced it for the economic cost of conflict in the Basque Country; Abadie, Diamond and Hainmueller (2010) formalised it.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> What Makes It Credible</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Pre-period fit</div><div class="definition-text">The synthetic unit must track the treated unit closely for many pre-intervention periods. A poor pre-fit makes the post-period gap meaningless.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Donor pool discipline</div><div class="definition-text">Donors must be plausibly unaffected by the treatment and not subject to their own shocks at the same time.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Placebo inference</div><div class="definition-text">Re-run the method pretending each donor was treated. If the real treated unit’s gap is extreme relative to the placebo gaps, the effect is unlikely to be chance (Abadie 2021).</div></div></div>
<p>A natural Indian application is a single-state policy shock, such as Bihar’s 2016 alcohol prohibition, where a synthetic Bihar built from other states provides the counterfactual for outcomes like consumption, crime, or household spending.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>Inference is by placebo, not the usual standard error.</strong> With one treated unit there is no sampling distribution in the ordinary sense. Credibility comes from the pre-period fit and from showing the treated gap stands out against placebo runs on the donors.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>A good post-period gap with a poor pre-period fit is worthless.</strong> Always show the pre-intervention tracking plot. If the synthetic unit cannot reproduce the treated unit’s history, it cannot stand in for its counterfactual future.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">Synthetic control sells itself in a single chart: the real line and the synthetic line on top of each other before the policy, then splitting after. When the pre-period fit is genuinely tight, it is the most persuasive quasi-experimental picture I know for a single-unit reform.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: synthetic control with placebo inference
library(tidysynth)
sc &lt;- df %&gt;% synthetic_control(outcome = y, unit = state, time = year,
        i_unit = "Bihar", i_time = 2016) %&gt;%
        generate_predictor(...) %&gt;% generate_weights() %&gt;% generate_control()
plot_trends(sc); plot_placebos(sc)</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Abadie &amp; Gardeazabal (2003), <em>AER</em> 93(1); Abadie, Diamond &amp; Hainmueller (2010), <em>JASA</em> 105(490); Abadie (2021), <em>JEL</em> 59(2).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie, Diamond &amp; Hainmueller (2010). "Synthetic Control Methods for Comparative Case Studies."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie (2021). "Using Synthetic Controls: Feasibility, Data Requirements, and Methodology." <em>JEL</em> 59(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie &amp; Gardeazabal (2003). "The Economic Costs of Conflict." <em>AER</em> 93(1).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm what makes a synthetic control credible.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Synthetic control is most useful when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1a" value="a"><label for="m9q1a">You have hundreds of treated units.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1b" value="b"><label for="m9q1b">A single unit is treated and no obvious comparison exists.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1c" value="c"><label for="m9q1c">Treatment is randomly assigned.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1d" value="d"><label for="m9q1d">There is a sharp cutoff.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Inference in synthetic control typically relies on: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2a" value="a"><label for="m9q2a">Clustered standard errors.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2b" value="b"><label for="m9q2b">A first-stage F-statistic.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2c" value="c"><label for="m9q2c">Placebo runs treating each donor as if it were treated.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2d" value="d"><label for="m9q2d">The McCrary test.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A poor pre-intervention fit means: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3a" value="a"><label for="m9q3a">The post-period gap cannot be interpreted as the effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3b" value="b"><label for="m9q3b">The effect is larger.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3c" value="c"><label for="m9q3c">The donor pool is too small only.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3d" value="d"><label for="m9q3d">Parallel trends holds.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>For a single-state or single-city reform you know, which other units would you put in the donor pool, and which would you exclude because they faced their own shock at the same time?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 10, 'Module 10: Causal Machine Learning',
  'Estimating heterogeneous effects without fishing, causal forests and double machine learning, and the firm line that ML supplies flexibility, not identification.',
  $html$<h2>Module 10: Causal Machine Learning</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">The toolkit so far estimates one average effect. Often the question is sharper: for whom does the programme work, and how do we target the next rupee? Machine learning, bent to causal questions, estimates effects that vary across people without overfitting them into noise.</p></div>
<h3>From Average to Heterogeneous Effects</h3>
<p>A cash transfer or an input subsidy rarely helps everyone equally. The conditional average treatment effect, CATE, is the effect as a function of characteristics: <em>E</em>[<em>Y</em>(1) &minus; <em>Y</em>(0) | <em>X</em> = <em>x</em>]. Estimating it by hand invites fishing: slice the data enough ways and a spurious subgroup effect always appears.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Two Tools That Discipline the Search</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Causal forests</div><div class="definition-text">Wager and Athey (2018) adapt random forests to estimate the CATE, with honest sample-splitting so the data used to find subgroups is not the data used to estimate their effects. This guards against the fishing problem.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Double machine learning</div><div class="definition-text">Chernozhukov et al. (2018). Use flexible learners for the nuisance parts (the outcome model and the treatment model), then combine them so the treatment-effect estimate stays valid and its confidence interval is honest, even with many covariates.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>These methods estimate heterogeneity; they do not create identification.</strong> A causal forest on observational data still needs unconfoundedness. The machine learning handles flexibility and many covariates. It does not rescue a design where the selection-bias term is non-zero.</p></div>
<p>The practitioner payoff is targeting. If a programme’s effect concentrates in a definable group, a causal forest can rank households by predicted benefit, and a budget can be aimed where it does the most good rather than spread evenly. That is the difference between an evaluation that scores a programme and one that improves it.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Honest splitting is the whole point.</strong> Finding subgroups and estimating their effects on the same data is the oldest way to manufacture a false positive. The value of causal forests is the discipline, not the flexibility. A subgroup result without out-of-sample validation is a hypothesis, not a finding.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">Funders increasingly ask "who benefits most" so they can target. Causal ML gives a credible answer, but only on a credible design. I am blunt with teams: a forest on a confounded comparison is a confounded answer with better graphics. Get identification right first, then let the machine find the heterogeneity.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: causal forest for heterogeneous effects and targeting
library(grf)
cf &lt;- causal_forest(X = covariates, Y = outcome, W = treat)
tau_hat &lt;- predict(cf)$predictions      # estimated CATE per household
best_linear_projection(cf, covariates)    # which covariates drive the effect</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Wager &amp; Athey (2018), <em>JASA</em> 113(523); Athey &amp; Imbens (2016), <em>PNAS</em> 113(27); Chernozhukov et al. (2018), <em>Econometrics Journal</em> 21(1); Künzel et al. (2019), <em>PNAS</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Athey &amp; Imbens (2019). "Machine Learning Methods Economists Should Know About." <em>Annual Review of Economics</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Wager &amp; Athey (2018). "Estimation and Inference of Heterogeneous Treatment Effects using Random Forests."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Chernozhukov et al. (2018). "Double/Debiased Machine Learning."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm what causal ML does and does not provide.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The CATE is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1a" value="a"><label for="m10q1a">The average effect over everyone.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1b" value="b"><label for="m10q1b">The treatment effect as a function of covariates, E[Y(1)-Y(0)|X].</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1c" value="c"><label for="m10q1c">The first-stage coefficient.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1d" value="d"><label for="m10q1d">The propensity score.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Causal forests guard against false subgroup findings mainly through: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2a" value="a"><label for="m10q2a">Larger samples.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2b" value="b"><label for="m10q2b">Stronger instruments.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2c" value="c"><label for="m10q2c">Honest sample-splitting between finding and estimating subgroups.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2d" value="d"><label for="m10q2d">Parallel trends.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Applying a causal forest to a confounded observational comparison gives: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3a" value="a"><label for="m10q3a">A confounded answer, because ML does not supply identification.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3b" value="b"><label for="m10q3b">An unbiased ATE.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3c" value="c"><label for="m10q3c">A valid instrument.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3d" value="d"><label for="m10q3d">A sharp RD.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>If a funder asked you to target a programme to the households that gain most, what covariates might drive heterogeneity, and what design would you need before a causal forest’s ranking could be trusted?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 11, 'Module 11: Reading and Interrogating an Evaluation',
  'A deliberate reading order and standing checklist, specification stability and publication bias, and the credibility debate (Banerjee-Duflo against Deaton and Pritchett) stated without false balance.',
  $html$<h2>Module 11: Reading and Interrogating an Evaluation</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Most practitioners will commission and read far more impact studies than they will ever run. This module is about the harder skill: holding a finished evaluation up to the light and deciding how much weight it can bear.</p></div>
<h3>The Reading Order</h3>
<p>Read an impact evaluation in a deliberate sequence, not front to back. First the estimand: what effect, on whom. Then the identification strategy and its assumption, stated in one sentence. Then the threats specific to that design, from the relevant module. Only then the results table, and last the abstract, which is where authors are most generous to themselves.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> A Standing Checklist</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Identification</div><div class="definition-text">Which design, which assumption, and is the assumption argued or merely asserted.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The specific threat</div><div class="definition-text">RCT: compliance, attrition, spillovers. IV: exclusion, first-stage strength. RD: manipulation. DiD: pre-trends and staggered timing.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Specification stability</div><div class="definition-text">Does the result survive sensible alternative specifications, or only one. A specification curve (Simonsohn, Simmons &amp; Nelson 2020) shows the whole distribution rather than the author’s favourite point.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Generalisation</div><div class="definition-text">To what population and setting does the number plausibly travel, and on what argument.</div></div></div>
<h3>The Credibility Debate, Without the False Balance</h3>
<p>The randomised-evaluation movement, associated with Banerjee and Duflo (2011) and recognised by the 2019 Nobel, sharpened development economics by insisting on credible identification. Deaton (2010, <em>JEL</em>) and Pritchett and Sandefur (2015) pressed the obvious limit: an internally valid estimate from one trial does not tell you what the programme will do elsewhere, and mechanically transporting RCT results across contexts can mislead more than a context-specific observational study.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> My read</div><p>The critics are right about external validity and wrong if taken to dismiss the method. The discipline of stating an identifying assumption is a permanent gain, and the field is better for it. The honest position is narrow internal claims plus an explicit, falsifiable argument about transport, rather than either RCT triumphalism or a retreat to unidentified storytelling.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Publication bias is structural, not occasional.</strong> Significant, surprising results are likelier to be published (Andrews &amp; Kasy 2019). A single striking finding, especially an early one, is weaker than it looks until it replicates. Weight a literature, not a paper.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">When I review an evaluation for a board, I write the identifying assumption in one sentence at the top of my note, then ask whether the paper earns it. If the assumption needs a paragraph of hedging, that is the finding. Most of my value to a funder is killing the over-claim before it reaches a decision.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Deaton (2010), <em>JEL</em> 48(2); Banerjee &amp; Duflo (2009, 2011); Pritchett &amp; Sandefur (2015), <em>J. Development Studies</em>; Andrews &amp; Kasy (2019), <em>AER</em>; Simonsohn, Simmons &amp; Nelson (2020), <em>Nature Human Behaviour</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Deaton, A. (2010). "Instruments, Randomization, and Learning about Development." <em>JEL</em> 48(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Pritchett &amp; Sandefur (2015). "Learning from Experiments When Context Matters."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Banerjee &amp; Duflo (2009). "The Experimental Approach to Development Economics."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm how to read a study and where the credibility debate actually lands.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> When reading an impact evaluation, you should first establish: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1a" value="a"><label for="m11q1a">The abstract’s headline.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1b" value="b"><label for="m11q1b">The estimand and the identifying assumption.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1c" value="c"><label for="m11q1c">The journal’s ranking.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1d" value="d"><label for="m11q1d">The sample size.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> The external-validity critique of RCTs (Deaton; Pritchett &amp; Sandefur) holds that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2a" value="a"><label for="m11q2a">An internally valid effect from one trial need not transport to other contexts.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2b" value="b"><label for="m11q2b">RCTs are never internally valid.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2c" value="c"><label for="m11q2c">Observational methods are always better.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2d" value="d"><label for="m11q2d">Randomisation introduces selection bias.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A specification curve is used to: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3a" value="a"><label for="m11q3a">Test the first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3b" value="b"><label for="m11q3b">Match treated and control units.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3c" value="c"><label for="m11q3c">Show the distribution of estimates across reasonable specifications, not one chosen result.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3d" value="d"><label for="m11q3d">Build a synthetic control.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take an evaluation you have cited in your own work. Write its identifying assumption in one sentence. Does the study earn that assumption, and how far would you let the result travel?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 12, 'Module 12: When There Is No Counterfactual',
  'Credible evaluation without a comparison group: contribution analysis, process tracing, realist CMO, and QCA, and the standard of stating what would disconfirm the claim.',
  $html$<h2>Module 12: When There Is No Counterfactual</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Much of development work cannot be evaluated with a control group. Advocacy, institutional reform, network building, and one-off national programmes have no comparison units and no clean before-and-after. Refusing to evaluate these is not an option. This module is the credible toolkit for the no-counterfactual world.</p></div>
<h3>Theory-Based Evaluation</h3>
<p>When you cannot estimate a counterfactual, you test the causal story instead. Lay out the theory of change as a chain of claims, then gather evidence for each link and, harder, against plausible rival explanations. The credibility comes from the rigour of the testing, not from a comparison group.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Four Credible Approaches</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Contribution analysis</div><div class="definition-text">Mayne (2008). Build the results chain, assemble evidence for each link, and seriously assess other factors that could explain the outcome. The deliverable is a contribution story that survives scrutiny, not an effect size.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Process tracing</div><div class="definition-text">Collier (2011). Use within-case evidence as diagnostic tests. A hoop test must be passed for a claim to survive; a smoking-gun test, if passed, strongly confirms it. Bayesian process tracing updates belief on each piece of evidence.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Realist evaluation</div><div class="definition-text">Pawson &amp; Tilley (1997). Ask what works, for whom, in what circumstances, through the context-mechanism-outcome configuration. The question is the mechanism, not the average.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Qualitative comparative analysis</div><div class="definition-text">Ragin (1987). Across a medium number of cases, identify which combinations of conditions are necessary or sufficient for the outcome, using set theory rather than correlation.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>No counterfactual is not the same as no rigour.</strong> A well-executed contribution analysis or process trace can be more credible than a badly identified quasi-experiment. The standard is the same: state the claim, state what would disconfirm it, and go looking for the disconfirming evidence honestly.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The misuse to avoid.</strong> These methods are sometimes chosen not because a counterfactual is impossible but because it is inconvenient. That is a problem. Reserve theory-based designs for cases where a counterfactual genuinely cannot exist, and say plainly when you are using them as a fallback rather than a first choice.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">When a funder wants impact numbers for an advocacy programme, I do not invent a counterfactual that cannot exist. I offer a contribution analysis with explicit rival explanations and a process trace of the key decisions. Most funders respect the honesty once they see the alternative is a fabricated comparison.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<p>These approaches connect back to the MEL flagship’s distinction between attribution and contribution. Modules 1 to 10 are about attribution, isolating a programme’s effect against a counterfactual. This module is about contribution, defensible reasoning when isolation is impossible.</p>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Mayne (2008), ILAC Brief 16; Collier (2011), <em>PS: Political Science &amp; Politics</em> 44(4); Pawson &amp; Tilley (1997), <em>Realistic Evaluation</em>; Ragin (1987), <em>The Comparative Method</em>; White &amp; Phillips (2012), 3ie Working Paper.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Mayne, J. (2008). "Contribution Analysis: An Approach to Exploring Cause and Effect."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Collier, D. (2011). "Understanding Process Tracing." <em>PS</em> 44(4).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Befani &amp; Stedman-Bryce (2017). "Process Tracing and Bayesian Updating for Impact Evaluation." <em>Evaluation</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm you can match a no-counterfactual method to its logic.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Contribution analysis (Mayne 2008) builds credibility by: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1a" value="a"><label for="m12q1a">Randomising participants.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1b" value="b"><label for="m12q1b">Assembling evidence for each link in the results chain and assessing rival explanations.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1c" value="c"><label for="m12q1c">Matching on the propensity score.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1d" value="d"><label for="m12q1d">Plotting parallel trends.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In process tracing, a "smoking-gun" test: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2a" value="a"><label for="m12q2a">Strongly confirms a claim if the evidence is found.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2b" value="b"><label for="m12q2b">Must be passed for a claim to survive.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2c" value="c"><label for="m12q2c">Tests the first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2d" value="d"><label for="m12q2d">Checks covariate balance.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Theory-based evaluation is the appropriate choice when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3a" value="a"><label for="m12q3a">A control group is available but inconvenient.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3b" value="b"><label for="m12q3b">You want a precise effect size.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3c" value="c"><label for="m12q3c">A counterfactual genuinely cannot be constructed, as in national advocacy.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3d" value="d"><label for="m12q3d">Treatment is randomly assigned.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Name a programme in your portfolio that has no possible counterfactual. Which of the four approaches fits it best, and what single piece of evidence would most strongly disconfirm its main causal claim?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 13, 'Module 13: Capstone Project',
  'Design and defend a credible evaluation for a real causal claim: estimand, design, identifying assumption, threats and checks, data and code, honest limits, and a one-page brief.',
  $html$<h2>Module 13: Capstone Project</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Pick a real causal claim, one you might actually need to defend to a funder or a board, and build the case for the design that can bear it. The capstone is not an exam. It is the deliverable this whole course exists to make you capable of producing.</p></div>
<h3>The Brief</h3>
<p>Choose a programme or policy and a specific causal claim about it. State the estimand precisely, name the design you would use to recover it, defend the identifying assumption, list the threats and how you would check each, and say honestly what the evidence could and could not establish. If no counterfactual is possible, build the theory-based case from Module 12 instead and say why.</p>
<div class="capstone-box"><div class="capstone-header"><div class="capstone-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 19v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2zm0 0V9a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v10m-6 0a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2h-2a2 2 0 01-2-2z"/></svg></div><h4 class="capstone-title">Design and Defend a Credible Evaluation</h4></div><div class="capstone-timeline"><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">1</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Claim and estimand</h5><p>State the causal question in one sentence and the estimand in potential-outcomes terms. Is it an ATT, an ATE, a LATE, a local effect at a cutoff? Justify which one the decision actually needs.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">2</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Design and identifying assumption</h5><p>Choose the design from Modules 4 to 10, or the theory-based route from Module 12. Write the identifying assumption in one sentence and defend it. Draw the DAG.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">3</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Threats and checks</h5><p>List the threats specific to your design and the diagnostic for each: balance, first-stage F, McCrary test, pre-trends and event study, placebo runs, stability across specifications.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">4</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Data and code plan</h5><p>Specify the data you need, the unit and level of analysis, the sample size or power, and the estimator. Sketch the R or Stata you would run.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">5</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Honest limits and a one-page brief</h5><p>State what the result could and could not establish, and how far it would travel. Distil the whole thing into a one-page brief a non-technical decision-maker could act on.</p></div></div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> What good looks like</div><p>A strong capstone is honest about what the design cannot do. The weakest submissions over-claim. The strongest ones name the single assumption the whole estimate rests on, show how they would stress it, and tell the decision-maker plainly where the evidence stops. That judgement, not the cleverness of the method, is the mark of a practitioner who can be trusted with a research budget.</p></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">Bring your capstone to a coaching session and I will play the sceptical funder. We will find the weakest link in the identification together, before a real reviewer does. That single conversation has saved more proposals than any reading I could assign.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<p>When you finish, you will have done the thing this course is for: taken a claim someone wanted to make, and decided, with reasons you can defend, exactly how much weight it can carry.</p>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Synthesises Modules 1 to 12. Core references: Imbens &amp; Rubin (2015); Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009); Cunningham (2021), <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Cunningham (2021). <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em> (free online).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Gertler et al. (2016). <em>Impact Evaluation in Practice</em>, 2nd ed. (World Bank).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Glennerster &amp; Takavarasha (2013). <em>Running Randomized Evaluations</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">A short self-check on the capstone standard before you submit.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The single most important sentence in your capstone is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1a" value="a"><label for="m13q1a">The literature review.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1b" value="b"><label for="m13q1b">The identifying assumption, stated plainly and defended.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1c" value="c"><label for="m13q1c">The sample size.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1d" value="d"><label for="m13q1d">The abstract.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A strong capstone is distinguished mainly by: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2a" value="a"><label for="m13q2a">Honesty about what the design cannot establish.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2b" value="b"><label for="m13q2b">The most advanced estimator available.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2c" value="c"><label for="m13q2c">The largest dataset.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2d" value="d"><label for="m13q2d">The most citations.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> If no counterfactual is possible for your chosen programme, you should: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3a" value="a"><label for="m13q3a">Abandon the evaluation.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3b" value="b"><label for="m13q3b">Invent a comparison group anyway.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3c" value="c"><label for="m13q3c">Build a theory-based case (contribution analysis or process tracing) and say why.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3d" value="d"><label for="m13q3d">Use a causal forest.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Before you start, write the one-sentence causal claim and the one-sentence identifying assumption your capstone will defend. If you cannot write the second sentence cleanly, that is the part to work on first.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false);
